#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""shadowfetch-doctor - structured diagnosis, support bundles, release check.

WHY THIS EXISTS AND WHY IT IS NOT shadowfetch-health
----------------------------------------------------
``shadowfetch-health`` answers "is this machine unwell right now" in eight
lines of printf. ``shadowfetch-facts`` answers "what hardware is this".
``shadowfetch-passport`` answers "will Shadowfetch run here" before install.
None of them can answer the question support actually receives, which is
"the sandbox will not start" / "the agent cannot reach its provider" /
"apt stopped working" -- because none of them looks at Firebreak, the
credential broker boundary, the provider registry, the APT trust chain or
the difference between what this release DECLARED and what the kernel is
actually ENFORCING.

This tool answers that question, and it does it in a shape a machine can
read, because the eventual consumer is a bug report and a release gate, not
a human squinting at colour codes.

THE ONE RULE THAT MATTERS MOST HERE
-----------------------------------
A check that could not be performed reports SKIP, never PASS.

A diagnostic tool that prints a green tick when it could not find the thing
it was supposed to inspect is worse than no tool: it converts ignorance into
a positive assurance, and someone then ships on it. Every collector in this
file distinguishes:

    PASS  the thing was inspected and is as it should be
    WARN  inspected, works, but is degraded or unusual
    FAIL  inspected, and it is wrong
    INFO  inspected; a fact worth reporting that is not a verdict
    SKIP  NOT inspected -- missing tool, no permission, not applicable

and ``--release-check`` treats SKIP as a failure, because a release may not
be signed off on facts nobody established.

TRUSTED PATHS (permanent invariant, do not relax)
-------------------------------------------------
Every executable whose OUTPUT decides a security question here is invoked
through an explicit absolute path from ``TRUSTED_TOOLS`` and is classified
before it runs. There is no ``shutil.which`` in this file and no PATH lookup
anywhere, and ``test_doctor.py`` asserts that by reading this source.

This is not theoretical. ``journalctl`` resolved through a user-writable
PATH can forge a clean audit; ``systemctl --failed`` resolved the same way
can report an empty list on a machine where everything is down; a
``nvidia-smi`` in ``/usr/local/bin`` can claim any driver version it likes.
When a tool does not classify as distro-managed, the check that needed it
reports SKIP with the reason. It never falls back to a PATH lookup and it
never reports PASS on an unverifiable answer.

The classification is deliberately a second implementation of the one in
``sf_providers.classify_executable``. It is NOT a second redactor -- see
below -- and the duplication is bounded and tested: ``test_doctor.py``
imports the missions module when it is present and asserts the two agree on
a fixture set, so they cannot drift. The copy exists because a diagnostic
tool has to work on a machine where ``shadowfetch-missions`` is broken,
half-installed or absent, which is exactly when someone runs a doctor.

SUPPORT BUNDLES: PRIVACY IS THE DESIGN, NOT A FILTER ON TOP
-----------------------------------------------------------
``--support`` writes a tar.gz for a bug report. Three structural controls,
in this order:

  1. ALLOWLIST. A collector may only read CONTENT from a path under
     ``COLLECT_ROOTS`` (plus ``HOME_READ_SUBPATHS``). ``DENY_PREFIXES`` is a
     second, redundant refusal for paths that must never be touched even if
     someone later widens the allowlist by accident. Metadata probes -- does
     this exist, what mode is it -- are governed separately and more loosely,
     because "is there a broker binary" has to be answerable and answering it
     exposes nothing; the home-directory rule and the never-collect list hold
     for probes too. Both policies live in ``Host``; there is no other
     file-reading path in the bundle builder.
  2. ONE WRITE PATH. Everything entering the archive goes through
     ``Bundle.add``, which applies the shared redactor to every byte with no
     opt-out, including the MANIFEST itself.
  3. FAIL CLOSED. If the shared redactor cannot be loaded from a trusted
     location, ``--support`` REFUSES to write a bundle. It does not fall
     back to a weaker local redactor, because a second redactor is exactly
     the thing that drifts and then silently stops catching a shape the
     real one catches. ``sf_redact`` is the one implementation; on a
     Shadowfetch install ``shadowfetch-desktop`` pulls in the package that
     ships it.

Environment variables contribute NAMES ONLY, never values, with a narrow
allowlist of posture variables whose values are informative. That mirrors
``sf_redact``'s reasoning: credential IDENTITIES are the audit trail and
must survive; credential VALUES must not exist here at all.

The MANIFEST lists exactly what was collected, what was skipped and why,
and the standing refusals. Hashes in it are of the bytes actually in the
archive -- that is, post-redaction -- so the manifest describes the file
the user is about to send, not a file that never existed.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime
import hashlib
import io
import json
import os
import pwd
import re
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tarfile
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Mapping, Sequence

VERSION = "1.0"
SCHEMA_VERSION = 1

# Same file every other Shadowfetch tool reports from, so a bug report cannot
# cite a version the system itself disagrees with.
VERSION_FILE = "/usr/share/shadowfetch/version"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Executable trust. See the module docstring: this decides whether an answer
# is admissible, not merely whether a program exists.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

class Trust:
    """What a program's location proves about who is able to change it.

    Value-identical to ``sf_providers.ExecutableTrust`` on purpose; the test
    suite pins the agreement so the two cannot drift into different verdicts
    for the same file.
    """

    DISTRO_MANAGED = "distro-managed"   # packaged path, root-owned all the way up
    USER_MANAGED = "user-managed"       # this user's own space, nobody else writable
    DEVELOPER = "developer"             # integrity fine, provenance unmanaged
    UNTRUSTED = "untrusted"             # somebody else can substitute it
    MISSING = "missing"                 # not there at all


PACKAGED_EXEC_PREFIXES = ("/usr/bin/", "/usr/sbin/", "/usr/libexec/",
                          "/usr/lib/", "/usr/local/lib/shadowfetch/",
                          "/bin/", "/sbin/", "/opt/")


def _private_group(gid: int, uid: int) -> bool:
    """True if gid is a per-user group whose only member is that user.

    Debian gives each user a group of their own name, so 0775 under $HOME is
    group-writable by a group of one. That is materially different from 0775
    under a shared group like ``staff`` or ``adm``, and the difference decides
    whether "group-writable" means a third party can swap the binary.
    """
    try:
        import grp
        user = pwd.getpwuid(uid)
        group = grp.getgrgid(gid)
    except (ImportError, KeyError, OSError):
        return False
    if set(group.gr_mem) - {user.pw_name}:
        return False
    return group.gr_gid == user.pw_gid or group.gr_name == user.pw_name


def _substitutable_by_others(info: os.stat_result, uid: int):
    """Why a third party could replace this inode, or None if none could."""
    mode = info.st_mode
    if info.st_uid not in (0, uid):
        return "owned by uid %d" % info.st_uid
    if stat.S_ISDIR(mode) and mode & stat.S_ISVTX:
        # Sticky: only an entry's own owner may replace it, so a world-writable
        # /tmp does not let anyone substitute another user's file.
        return None
    if mode & 0o002:
        return "world-writable"
    if mode & 0o020 and not _private_group(info.st_gid, uid):
        return "writable by shared group gid %d" % info.st_gid
    return None


def classify_executable(path, *, uid: int | None = None) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Return (Trust value, human reason) for a program.

    The walk covers every directory above the file as well as the file itself:
    replacing a directory entry is as good as replacing its target, so a check
    that stops at the file's own mode misses the more likely attack.
    """
    uid = os.getuid() if uid is None else uid
    try:
        resolved = Path(path).resolve()
    except OSError as exc:
        return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s cannot be resolved: %s" % (path, exc)
    if not resolved.is_absolute():
        return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s is not an absolute path" % resolved
    try:
        info = resolved.stat()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return Trust.MISSING, "%s does not exist" % resolved
    except OSError as exc:
        return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s cannot be inspected: %s" % (resolved, exc)
    if not stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
        return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s is not a regular file" % resolved
    if not os.access(str(resolved), os.X_OK):
        return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s is not executable" % resolved

    root_owned_throughout = True
    for component in (resolved, *resolved.parents):
        try:
            cinfo = component.stat()
        except OSError as exc:
            return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s cannot be inspected: %s" % (component, exc)
        problem = _substitutable_by_others(cinfo, uid)
        if problem is not None:
            return (Trust.UNTRUSTED,
                    "%s is %s, so the program can be substituted by someone "
                    "other than root or the invoking user" % (component, problem))
        if cinfo.st_uid != 0:
            root_owned_throughout = False

    if str(resolved).startswith(PACKAGED_EXEC_PREFIXES):
        if root_owned_throughout:
            return (Trust.DISTRO_MANAGED,
                    "%s is root-owned throughout a packaging-owned directory" % resolved)
        return (Trust.USER_MANAGED,
                "%s is in a packaging-owned directory but part of its path is "
                "owned by uid %d rather than root" % (resolved, uid))
    if root_owned_throughout:
        return (Trust.DEVELOPER,
                "%s is root-owned and nobody else can write it, but it is outside "
                "the packaging-owned directories, so nothing vouches for where it "
                "came from" % resolved)
    return Trust.USER_MANAGED, "%s is writable only by root and uid %d" % (resolved, uid)


#: Every executable this tool may run, by logical name, with the ABSOLUTE
#: candidate paths tried in order. There is no PATH lookup: a program that is
#: not at one of these paths does not exist as far as this tool is concerned,
#: and the check that wanted it reports SKIP.
#:
#: Every entry requires DISTRO_MANAGED. That is not excessive caution -- each
#: of these answers a question this tool then reports as fact, and a program a
#: third party can substitute can answer anything. A user-managed nvidia-smi
#: gets us "GPU state unverifiable", which is a true statement; accepting it
#: would get us a false one.
TRUSTED_TOOLS: dict[str, tuple[str, ...]] = {
    "systemctl": ("/usr/bin/systemctl", "/bin/systemctl"),
    "journalctl": ("/usr/bin/journalctl", "/bin/journalctl"),
    "dpkg": ("/usr/bin/dpkg", "/bin/dpkg"),
    "dpkg-query": ("/usr/bin/dpkg-query", "/bin/dpkg-query"),
    "bwrap": ("/usr/bin/bwrap", "/bin/bwrap"),
    "slirp4netns": ("/usr/bin/slirp4netns", "/bin/slirp4netns"),
    "nvidia-smi": ("/usr/bin/nvidia-smi",),
    "true": ("/usr/bin/true", "/bin/true"),
    "firebreak": ("/usr/bin/shadowfetch-firebreak",),
    "checkpoint": ("/usr/bin/shadowfetch-checkpoint",),
    "missions": ("/usr/bin/shadowfetch-missions",),
}

#: Required trust class per tool. Uniform today; kept as a table so a future
#: exception has to be written down here rather than argued at the call site.
REQUIRED_TRUST = {name: (Trust.DISTRO_MANAGED,) for name in TRUSTED_TOOLS}


@dataclass
class Tool:
    name: str
    path: str | None
    trust: str
    reason: str

    @property
    def usable(self) -> bool:
        return self.path is not None and self.trust in REQUIRED_TRUST[self.name]


def resolve_tool(name: str) -> Tool:
    """First candidate absolute path that classifies acceptably, else why not.

    Candidates are tried in order and the first one that exists is the answer,
    even when it classifies badly. Skipping a badly-classified /usr/bin/foo to
    "find" an acceptable /bin/foo would report on a program that is not the one
    that would actually run -- a more misleading answer than the bad one.
    """
    for candidate in TRUSTED_TOOLS[name]:
        trust, reason = classify_executable(candidate)
        if trust == Trust.MISSING:
            continue
        return Tool(name, candidate, trust, reason)
    return Tool(name, None, Trust.MISSING,
                "none of %s exists" % ", ".join(TRUSTED_TOOLS[name]))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Findings
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

PASS, WARN, FAIL, INFO, SKIP = "pass", "warn", "fail", "info", "skip"
STATUSES = (PASS, WARN, FAIL, INFO, SKIP)

CATEGORIES = ("system", "gpu", "drivers", "ai", "firebreak", "broker",
              "apt", "services", "recovery", "security", "development")


@dataclass
class Evidence:
    """Where a finding's claim came from. Never decoration.

    A doctor that cannot show its source produces advice nobody can check,
    and the person receiving it has to trust the tool instead of the reading.
    """

    kind: str            # "file" | "exec" | "probe" | "none"
    source: str          # absolute path, or the exact argv
    ok: bool
    note: str = ""
    trust: str = ""      # executable trust class, for kind == "exec"

    def as_dict(self) -> dict:
        out = {"kind": self.kind, "source": self.source, "ok": self.ok}
        if self.note:
            out["note"] = self.note
        if self.trust:
            out["trust"] = self.trust
        return out


@dataclass
class Finding:
    id: str
    category: str
    title: str
    status: str
    summary: str
    detail: dict = field(default_factory=dict)
    remedy: str = ""
    evidence: list[Evidence] = field(default_factory=list)
    #: True when this finding participates in --release-check. A release may
    #: not be signed off on a SKIP, so gated checks that could not run fail
    #: the gate rather than being quietly dropped.
    gated: bool = False

    def as_dict(self) -> dict:
        out = {
            "id": self.id,
            "category": self.category,
            "title": self.title,
            "status": self.status,
            "summary": self.summary,
            "gated": self.gated,
        }
        if self.detail:
            out["detail"] = self.detail
        if self.remedy:
            out["remedy"] = self.remedy
        if self.evidence:
            out["evidence"] = [item.as_dict() for item in self.evidence]
        return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Host access. Every read and every exec in this file goes through here, which
# is what makes the allowlist a control rather than a convention.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

#: Prefixes a collector may read. Anything else raises. Widening this list is
#: a privacy decision and should be argued in review, which is why it is one
#: table rather than a habit of passing absolute paths around.
COLLECT_ROOTS = (
    "/etc/os-release",
    "/etc/apt/",
    "/etc/sysctl.d/",
    "/etc/modprobe.d/",
    "/etc/shadowfetch/",
    "/proc/",
    "/sys/",
    "/usr/share/shadowfetch/",
    "/usr/share/keyrings/",
    "/usr/lib/shadowfetch/",
    "/var/lib/apt/lists/",
    "/var/log/journal",
    # Existence-only reads. Nothing collects content from either: /.snapshots is
    # listed to count rollback points, /usr/local/bin is probed because a copy
    # there shadows the packaged tool on a default PATH.
    "/.snapshots",
    "/usr/local/bin/",
)

#: Refused even if a future edit widens COLLECT_ROOTS to cover them. These are
#: the paths whose exposure in a support bundle would be the incident. The
#: overlap with COLLECT_ROOTS being empty today is the point: this is the
#: second lock, not the first.
DENY_PREFIXES = (
    "/etc/shadow",
    "/etc/gshadow",
    "/etc/ssl/private/",
    "/etc/ssh/",
    "/root/",
    "/var/lib/sudo/",
    "/proc/self/environ",
    "/proc/1/environ",
)

#: Inside a home directory, these are the only subtrees this tool may look at
#: AT ALL -- even to ask whether a file exists. Everything else under a home
#: -- dotfiles, keys, browser profiles, shell history, documents -- is out of
#: scope by construction, not by a pattern match someone has to keep current.
HOME_PROBE_SUBPATHS = (
    ".local/state/shadowfetch",
)

#: And of those, the only subtree whose CONTENT may be read. Metadata (does it
#: exist, what mode is it, how big) is a weaker exposure than content and is
#: what the permission and audit-directory checks need; content is what ends up
#: in a support bundle. Keeping them as two lists means widening one does not
#: silently widen the other.
HOME_READ_SUBPATHS = (
    ".local/state/shadowfetch/firebreak",
)


class Refused(Exception):
    """A collector asked for a path the privacy policy does not permit."""


@dataclass
class Outcome:
    ok: bool
    stdout: str
    stderr: str
    code: int
    evidence: Evidence


class Host:
    """The machine under inspection.

    ``root`` exists so the support bundle can be built against a synthetic
    tree in a test without the shipped program growing an environment
    variable that relocates its reads. A relocatable diagnostic is a
    diagnostic someone can point at a directory they prepared.

    When ``root`` is not "/" no subprocess runs at all: there is no honest way
    to ask the real ``journalctl`` about a synthetic tree, so those collectors
    report SKIP with that reason instead of quietly describing this machine.
    """

    def __init__(self, root: str | Path = "/", env: Mapping[str, str] | None = None,
                 timeout: int = 8):
        self.root = Path(root).resolve()
        self.synthetic = self.root != Path("/")
        self.env = dict(os.environ if env is None else env)
        self.timeout = timeout
        self._tools: dict[str, Tool] = {}

    # ---- paths -------------------------------------------------------- #

    @staticmethod
    def _under(absolute: str, base: str) -> bool:
        return absolute == base.rstrip("/") or absolute.startswith(base.rstrip("/") + "/")

    def _probe_permitted(self, absolute: str) -> None:
        """May this tool look at this path's metadata at all?

        Weaker than the read policy on purpose: "does /usr/bin/shadowfetch-brokerd
        exist" is a question this tool must be able to ask, and answering it
        exposes nothing. The two rules that hold here regardless are the
        never-collect list and the home-directory rule -- a diagnostic tool has
        no business stat()ing someone's Documents folder either.
        """
        if not absolute.startswith("/"):
            raise Refused("%s is not an absolute path" % absolute)
        if ".." in Path(absolute).parts:
            raise Refused("%s contains a parent traversal" % absolute)
        for deny in DENY_PREFIXES:
            if self._under(absolute, deny):
                raise Refused("%s is on the never-collect list" % absolute)
        home = self.home()
        homes = [str(home)] if home is not None else []
        homes.append("/home")
        for base in homes:
            if not self._under(absolute, base):
                continue
            if home is not None and any(
                    self._under(absolute, str(home / leaf)) for leaf in HOME_PROBE_SUBPATHS):
                return
            raise Refused("%s is inside a home directory and outside the "
                          "Shadowfetch state subtree" % absolute)

    def _read_permitted(self, absolute: str) -> None:
        """May this tool read this path's CONTENT? Allowlist, not deny-list.

        Everything whose bytes could reach a support bundle comes through here.
        """
        self._probe_permitted(absolute)
        if absolute.startswith(COLLECT_ROOTS):
            return
        home = self.home()
        if home is not None and any(self._under(absolute, str(home / leaf))
                                    for leaf in HOME_READ_SUBPATHS):
            return
        raise Refused("%s is outside the collection allowlist" % absolute)

    def resolve(self, absolute: str) -> Path:
        """Map an absolute system path in, after the CONTENT policy check."""
        self._read_permitted(absolute)
        return self.root / absolute.lstrip("/")

    def probe_path(self, absolute: str) -> Path:
        """Map an absolute system path in, after the METADATA policy check."""
        self._probe_permitted(absolute)
        return self.root / absolute.lstrip("/")

    def home(self) -> Path | None:
        """This user's home from the passwd database, never from $HOME.

        $HOME is ambient -- a desktop session, a user unit or a container image
        sets it for reasons unrelated to this program. Firebreak resolves its
        audit directory from passwd for exactly this reason, and a tool that
        inspects that directory has to agree with it about where it is.
        """
        try:
            entry = pwd.getpwuid(os.geteuid())
        except KeyError:
            return None
        path = (entry.pw_dir or "").strip()
        if not path.startswith("/"):
            return None
        return Path(path)

    def read_text(self, absolute: str, limit: int = 1 << 20) -> tuple[str | None, Evidence]:
        path = self.resolve(absolute)
        try:
            with path.open("rb") as handle:
                data = handle.read(limit)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return None, Evidence("file", absolute, False, "does not exist")
        except PermissionError:
            return None, Evidence("file", absolute, False, "permission denied")
        except OSError as exc:
            return None, Evidence("file", absolute, False, str(exc))
        return (data.decode("utf-8", "replace"),
                Evidence("file", absolute, True, "%d bytes" % len(data)))

    def read_bytes(self, absolute: str, limit: int = 1 << 22) -> tuple[bytes | None, Evidence]:
        text, evidence = self.read_text(absolute, limit)
        return (None if text is None else text.encode("utf-8", "replace")), evidence

    # Refused deliberately PROPAGATES out of these. Swallowing it into a False
    # would report "that file does not exist" when the truth is "this tool
    # refused to look", and a diagnostic tool that confuses the two invents
    # findings. diagnose() turns an escaped Refused into a visible FAIL naming
    # the path, which is a bug report about this tool rather than about the
    # machine.
    def exists(self, absolute: str) -> bool:
        return self.probe_path(absolute).exists()

    def is_dir(self, absolute: str) -> bool:
        return self.probe_path(absolute).is_dir()

    def glob(self, absolute_dir: str, pattern: str) -> list[str]:
        base = self.probe_path(absolute_dir)
        if not base.is_dir():
            return []
        prefix = absolute_dir.rstrip("/")
        return sorted(prefix + "/" + child.name for child in base.glob(pattern))

    def stat(self, absolute: str) -> os.stat_result | None:
        """Metadata, or None when the path genuinely is not there."""
        try:
            return self.probe_path(absolute).lstat()
        except OSError:
            return None

    def readlink(self, absolute: str) -> str:
        """Link target basename, or "" -- metadata, so the probe policy applies."""
        try:
            return os.path.basename(os.readlink(str(self.probe_path(absolute))))
        except OSError:
            return ""

    # ---- executables --------------------------------------------------- #

    def tool(self, name: str) -> Tool:
        if name not in self._tools:
            self._tools[name] = resolve_tool(name)
        return self._tools[name]

    def _child_env(self) -> dict[str, str]:
        """A minimal environment for a trusted tool.

        The caller's environment is NOT passed through. It can hold credential
        values, and a diagnostic tool has no business handing those to a
        subprocess whose output it is about to print. PATH is set only because
        some tools re-exec helpers; nothing in this file resolves a program
        through it.
        """
        home = self.home()
        env = {"LC_ALL": "C", "LANG": "C", "PATH": "/usr/bin:/bin"}
        if home is not None:
            env["HOME"] = str(home)
        return env

    def run(self, name: str, args: Sequence[str], timeout: int | None = None) -> Outcome:
        """Run a trusted tool. Never falls back to a PATH lookup.

        A tool that is missing or does not classify as required produces
        ok=False with the reason, and the caller must turn that into SKIP.
        """
        argv_display = "%s %s" % (name, " ".join(args))
        if self.synthetic:
            return Outcome(False, "", "", -1,
                           Evidence("exec", argv_display, False,
                                    "not run: inspecting a synthetic root at %s" % self.root))
        chosen = self.tool(name)
        if chosen.path is None:
            return Outcome(False, "", "", -1,
                           Evidence("exec", argv_display, False,
                                    "no trusted path: " + chosen.reason, chosen.trust))
        if not chosen.usable:
            return Outcome(False, "", "", -1,
                           Evidence("exec", chosen.path, False,
                                    "refused, classifies as %s: %s" % (chosen.trust, chosen.reason),
                                    chosen.trust))
        argv = [chosen.path, *args]
        try:
            result = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True,
                                    timeout=self.timeout if timeout is None else timeout,
                                    check=False, env=self._child_env())
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
            return Outcome(False, "", "", -1,
                           Evidence("exec", " ".join(argv), False, str(exc), chosen.trust))
        return Outcome(result.returncode == 0, result.stdout.strip(), result.stderr.strip(),
                       result.returncode,
                       Evidence("exec", " ".join(argv), True,
                                "exit %d" % result.returncode, chosen.trust))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Small helpers shared by the checks
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def _kv(text: str) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Parse ``key=value`` / ``key: value`` config text, ignoring comments."""
    out: dict[str, str] = {}
    for line in text.splitlines():
        line = line.split("#", 1)[0].strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        for sep in ("=", ":"):
            if sep in line:
                key, value = line.split(sep, 1)
                out[key.strip()] = value.strip().strip('"')
                break
    return out


def _skip(id_, category, title, reason, evidence=None, gated=False) -> Finding:
    """The honest shape of "I could not look".

    Kept as a helper so that "not inspected" is one line at every call site
    and nobody is tempted to write PASS because the code path was awkward.

    A skip still carries provenance. "I could not look, and here is what I
    tried" is a checkable statement; "I could not look" on its own is not, and
    a reader has no way to tell a missing file from a bug in this tool.
    """
    return Finding(id=id_, category=category, title=title, status=SKIP,
                   summary="Not inspected: " + reason,
                   evidence=list(evidence) if evidence
                            else [Evidence("none", "no readable source", False, reason)],
                   gated=gated)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: system
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def check_system(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []

    text, ev = host.read_text("/proc/version")
    if text:
        out.append(Finding("sys.kernel", "system", "Kernel", INFO,
                           text.strip().split(" (")[0], evidence=[ev]))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("sys.kernel", "system", "Kernel", ev.note, [ev]))

    text, ev = host.read_text(VERSION_FILE)
    if text and text.strip():
        out.append(Finding("sys.release", "system", "Shadowfetch release", PASS,
                           text.strip(), detail={"version": text.strip()},
                           evidence=[ev], gated=True))
    else:
        out.append(Finding("sys.release", "system", "Shadowfetch release", FAIL,
                           "No release version at " + VERSION_FILE,
                           remedy="Reinstall shadowfetch-branding; every tool reports "
                                  "its version from this file.",
                           evidence=[ev], gated=True))

    # Disk. A full root is the single most common cause of "everything broke
    # at once" in support, and it breaks apt, snapshots and the sandbox alike.
    for mount in ("/", "/home", "/var"):
        try:
            usage = shutil.disk_usage(str(host.root / mount.lstrip("/")))
        except OSError as exc:
            out.append(_skip("sys.disk" + mount.replace("/", "."), "system",
                             "Disk %s" % mount, str(exc)))
            continue
        used = round(usage.used / usage.total * 100) if usage.total else 0
        free_gib = usage.free / 1024 ** 3
        status = FAIL if free_gib < 1 else (WARN if used >= 90 else PASS)
        out.append(Finding("sys.disk" + mount.replace("/", "."), "system",
                           "Disk %s" % mount, status,
                           "%d%% used, %.1f GiB free" % (used, free_gib),
                           detail={"percent_used": used, "free_bytes": usage.free},
                           remedy="Free space before an update or a sandbox run."
                                  if status != PASS else "",
                           evidence=[Evidence("probe", "statvfs " + mount, True)]))

    text, ev = host.read_text("/proc/meminfo")
    if text:
        meminfo = {k: int(v.split()[0]) for k, v in
                   (line.split(":", 1) for line in text.splitlines() if ":" in line)}
        available = meminfo.get("MemAvailable", 0) / 1024 ** 2
        total = meminfo.get("MemTotal", 0) / 1024 ** 2
        out.append(Finding("sys.memory", "system", "Memory",
                           WARN if available < 1 else PASS,
                           "%.1f GiB available of %.1f GiB" % (available, total),
                           detail={"available_gib": round(available, 2),
                                   "total_gib": round(total, 2)},
                           evidence=[ev]))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("sys.memory", "system", "Memory", ev.note, [ev]))

    text, ev = host.read_text("/proc/mounts")
    if text:
        rootfs = ""
        for line in text.splitlines():
            parts = line.split()
            if len(parts) >= 3 and parts[1] == "/":
                rootfs = parts[2]
                break
        out.append(Finding("sys.rootfs", "system", "Root filesystem", INFO,
                           rootfs or "unknown", detail={"fstype": rootfs},
                           evidence=[ev]))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("sys.rootfs", "system", "Root filesystem", ev.note, [ev]))

    # Clock. A wrong clock breaks TLS and makes a valid APT index look expired,
    # and the resulting symptom ("apt is broken") points nowhere near the cause.
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
    plausible = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc) <= now
    out.append(Finding("sys.clock", "system", "System clock",
                       PASS if plausible else FAIL,
                       now.isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
                       remedy="" if plausible else
                              "Set the clock: TLS and APT metadata validity both depend on it.",
                       evidence=[Evidence("probe", "clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)", True)]))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: GPU and drivers, read from sysfs rather than a parsed lspci
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

PCI_CLASS_DISPLAY = "0x03"


def _pci_devices(host: Host) -> list[dict]:
    devices = []
    for slot in host.glob("/sys/bus/pci/devices", "*"):
        entry = {"slot": slot.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]}
        for field_name in ("class", "vendor", "device"):
            value, _ = host.read_text(slot + "/" + field_name, 64)
            entry[field_name] = (value or "").strip()
        entry["driver"] = host.readlink(slot + "/driver")
        devices.append(entry)
    return devices


def check_gpu(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []
    devices = _pci_devices(host)
    if not devices:
        return [_skip("gpu.devices", "gpu", "Graphics devices",
                      "/sys/bus/pci/devices is empty or unreadable")]

    display = [d for d in devices if d.get("class", "").startswith(PCI_CLASS_DISPLAY)]
    out.append(Finding("gpu.devices", "gpu", "Graphics devices",
                       PASS if display else WARN,
                       "%d display device(s)" % len(display),
                       detail={"devices": [{"slot": d["slot"], "vendor": d["vendor"],
                                            "device": d["device"], "driver": d["driver"]}
                                           for d in display]},
                       evidence=[Evidence("file", "/sys/bus/pci/devices/*/{class,vendor,device,driver}", True)]))

    unbound = [d["slot"] for d in display if not d["driver"]]
    if display:
        out.append(Finding("gpu.driver", "gpu", "Graphics driver bound",
                           FAIL if unbound else PASS,
                           "no driver bound to %s" % ", ".join(unbound) if unbound
                           else "every display device has a kernel driver",
                           detail={"unbound": unbound},
                           remedy="Run shadowfetch-gpu." if unbound else "",
                           evidence=[Evidence("file", "/sys/bus/pci/devices/*/driver", True)]))

    modules, ev = host.read_text("/proc/modules")
    if modules is None:
        out.append(_skip("gpu.modules", "gpu", "Graphics kernel modules", ev.note, [ev]))
    else:
        loaded = {line.split()[0] for line in modules.splitlines() if line.split()}
        nvidia = "nvidia" in loaded
        nouveau = "nouveau" in loaded
        if nvidia and nouveau:
            status, summary = FAIL, "nvidia and nouveau are both loaded"
        elif nvidia:
            status, summary = PASS, "nvidia is loaded"
        elif nouveau:
            status, summary = INFO, "nouveau is loaded (open driver)"
        else:
            status, summary = INFO, "neither nvidia nor nouveau is loaded"
        out.append(Finding("gpu.modules", "gpu", "Graphics kernel modules", status, summary,
                           detail={"nvidia": nvidia, "nouveau": nouveau,
                                   "amdgpu": "amdgpu" in loaded, "i915": "i915" in loaded},
                           remedy="Blacklist nouveau and rebuild the initramfs."
                                  if (nvidia and nouveau) else "",
                           evidence=[ev]))

    # nvidia-smi is asked for a fact that is then reported; it is therefore
    # resolved through the trusted table and refused if substitutable.
    if any(d["vendor"] == "0x10de" for d in display):
        smi = host.run("nvidia-smi",
                       ["--query-gpu=name,driver_version,memory.total",
                        "--format=csv,noheader"])
        if smi.ok and smi.stdout:
            out.append(Finding("gpu.nvidia", "gpu", "NVIDIA runtime", PASS,
                               smi.stdout.splitlines()[0].strip(),
                               detail={"query": smi.stdout.splitlines()},
                               evidence=[smi.evidence]))
        else:
            out.append(Finding("gpu.nvidia", "gpu", "NVIDIA runtime",
                               SKIP if not smi.evidence.ok else FAIL,
                               "Not inspected: " + smi.evidence.note if not smi.evidence.ok
                               else "nvidia-smi failed: " + (smi.stderr or "no output"),
                               remedy="An NVIDIA display device is present but the "
                                      "userspace driver did not answer; run shadowfetch-gpu.",
                               evidence=[smi.evidence]))
    return out


def check_drivers(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []
    devices = _pci_devices(host)
    if devices:
        # Bridges and legacy classes have no driver by design; reporting them
        # would bury the two devices that actually matter in forty that do not.
        ignorable = ("0x06", "0x08", "0x0c05", "0x11")
        unbound = [d for d in devices
                   if not d["driver"] and not d.get("class", "").startswith(ignorable)]
        out.append(Finding("drv.unbound", "drivers", "PCI devices without a driver",
                           WARN if unbound else PASS,
                           "%d device(s) with no kernel driver" % len(unbound),
                           detail={"devices": [{"slot": d["slot"], "class": d["class"],
                                                "vendor": d["vendor"], "device": d["device"]}
                                               for d in unbound]},
                           remedy="Run shadowfetch-hardware for per-device advice."
                                  if unbound else "",
                           evidence=[Evidence("file", "/sys/bus/pci/devices/*/driver", True)]))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("drv.unbound", "drivers", "PCI devices without a driver",
                         "/sys/bus/pci/devices is empty or unreadable"))

    text, ev = host.read_text("/proc/sys/kernel/tainted", 64)
    if text is None:
        out.append(_skip("drv.tainted", "drivers", "Kernel taint", ev.note, [ev]))
    else:
        value = int(text.strip() or "0")
        # Bit 12 (OOT_MODULE) is set by every out-of-tree module including the
        # NVIDIA one, which is a supported configuration here, so a taint that
        # is ONLY that bit is not a warning.
        interesting = value & ~(1 << 12)
        out.append(Finding("drv.tainted", "drivers", "Kernel taint",
                           WARN if interesting else PASS,
                           "taint mask %d" % value, detail={"tainted": value},
                           remedy="Check dmesg for the module that tainted the kernel."
                                  if interesting else "",
                           evidence=[ev]))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: AI / provider readiness
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

MISSIONS_LIB = "/usr/lib/shadowfetch/missions"
PROVIDERS_DIR = "/usr/share/shadowfetch/providers.d"


def check_ai(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []

    present = host.is_dir(MISSIONS_LIB)
    out.append(Finding("ai.runtime", "ai", "Mission Control runtime",
                       PASS if present else FAIL,
                       "installed at " + MISSIONS_LIB if present
                       else "missing: " + MISSIONS_LIB,
                       remedy="" if present else "Install shadowfetch-missions.",
                       evidence=[Evidence("file", MISSIONS_LIB, present)],
                       gated=True))

    manifests = host.glob(PROVIDERS_DIR, "*.json")
    if host.is_dir(PROVIDERS_DIR):
        ids = []
        for path in manifests:
            text, _ = host.read_text(path, 1 << 18)
            try:
                ids.append(json.loads(text or "{}").get("id") or path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1])
            except ValueError:
                ids.append(path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] + " (unparseable)")
        out.append(Finding("ai.providers", "ai", "Provider manifests",
                           PASS if ids else WARN,
                           "%d manifest(s): %s" % (len(ids), ", ".join(ids) or "none"),
                           detail={"providers": ids},
                           remedy="" if ids else "No provider is registered; missions "
                                                 "cannot select one.",
                           evidence=[Evidence("file", PROVIDERS_DIR + "/*.json", True)]))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("ai.providers", "ai", "Provider manifests",
                         PROVIDERS_DIR + " does not exist",
                         [Evidence("file", PROVIDERS_DIR, False, "does not exist")]))

    # The orchestrator's own view. This is the only answer that reflects
    # manifest validation and the approved-provider policy rather than a
    # directory listing, which is why it is worth a subprocess.
    caps = host.run("missions", ["--json", "capabilities"], timeout=20)
    if caps.ok:
        try:
            document = json.loads(caps.stdout)
        except ValueError:
            document = {}
        out.append(Finding("ai.capabilities", "ai", "Orchestrator capabilities", PASS,
                           "shadowfetch-missions answered",
                           detail={"capabilities": document},
                           evidence=[caps.evidence]))
    else:
        out.append(Finding("ai.capabilities", "ai", "Orchestrator capabilities",
                           SKIP if not caps.evidence.ok else FAIL,
                           "Not inspected: " + caps.evidence.note if not caps.evidence.ok
                           else "shadowfetch-missions failed: " + (caps.stderr or "no output"),
                           evidence=[caps.evidence]))

    # Credential IDENTITIES only. Values are never read, never printed and
    # never collected -- see the module docstring. The identities ARE the
    # audit trail: "no provider credential is present" is the single most
    # common reason an agent run fails, and it is not a secret.
    identities = sorted(name for name in CREDENTIAL_IDENTITY_NAMES if host.env.get(name))
    out.append(Finding("ai.credentials", "ai", "Provider credentials in this environment",
                       INFO,
                       "%d credential identity/identities present" % len(identities),
                       detail={"identities": identities,
                               "note": "names only; this tool never reads a value"},
                       evidence=[Evidence("probe", "os.environ keys", True,
                                          "names only, no values")]))
    return out


#: Kept in step with the Firebreak grant list and sf_redact.CREDENTIAL_NAMES.
#: Names only -- this list is used to report WHICH identities exist, never to
#: read one.
CREDENTIAL_IDENTITY_NAMES = (
    "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY", "AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID", "AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY",
    "AWS_SESSION_TOKEN", "CF_API_TOKEN", "CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN", "CODEX_API_KEY",
    "DEEPSEEK_API_KEY", "DIGITALOCEAN_TOKEN", "DOCKER_PASSWORD", "GEMINI_API_KEY",
    "GH_TOKEN", "GITHUB_TOKEN", "GITLAB_TOKEN", "GOOGLE_API_KEY", "GROQ_API_KEY",
    "HF_TOKEN", "HUGGING_FACE_HUB_TOKEN", "MISTRAL_API_KEY", "NPM_TOKEN",
    "OPENAI_API_KEY", "OPENROUTER_API_KEY", "PYPI_TOKEN", "XAI_API_KEY",
)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: Firebreak enforcement
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def check_firebreak(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []

    for name, title, gated in (("firebreak", "Firebreak", True),
                               ("bwrap", "bubblewrap", True),
                               ("checkpoint", "Checkpoint engine", False)):
        tool = host.tool(name)
        if tool.path is None:
            out.append(Finding("fb." + name, "firebreak", title, FAIL,
                               "not installed at any trusted path",
                               detail={"candidates": list(TRUSTED_TOOLS[name])},
                               remedy="Install the package that ships it; without it "
                                      "no session can be confined.",
                               evidence=[Evidence("exec", "|".join(TRUSTED_TOOLS[name]),
                                                  False, tool.reason)],
                               gated=gated))
        elif not tool.usable:
            out.append(Finding("fb." + name, "firebreak", title, FAIL,
                               "classifies as %s, not distro-managed" % tool.trust,
                               detail={"path": tool.path, "trust": tool.trust},
                               remedy="A program a third party can substitute cannot be "
                                      "trusted to confine anything. Reinstall from the "
                                      "repository.",
                               evidence=[Evidence("exec", tool.path, False, tool.reason,
                                                  tool.trust)],
                               gated=gated))
        else:
            out.append(Finding("fb." + name, "firebreak", title, PASS,
                               "%s (%s)" % (tool.path, tool.trust),
                               detail={"path": tool.path, "trust": tool.trust},
                               evidence=[Evidence("exec", tool.path, True, tool.reason,
                                                  tool.trust)],
                               gated=gated))

    # Unprivileged user namespaces. This is THE reason a sandbox refuses to
    # start on an Ubuntu-derived kernel, and the error bwrap prints does not
    # say so.
    knobs = {}
    for knob in ("/proc/sys/kernel/unprivileged_userns_clone",
                 "/proc/sys/kernel/apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns",
                 "/proc/sys/user/max_user_namespaces"):
        text, _ = host.read_text(knob, 64)
        if text is not None:
            knobs[knob] = text.strip()
    blocked = (knobs.get("/proc/sys/kernel/unprivileged_userns_clone") == "0"
               or knobs.get("/proc/sys/kernel/apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns") == "1"
               or knobs.get("/proc/sys/user/max_user_namespaces") == "0")
    if knobs:
        out.append(Finding("fb.userns", "firebreak", "Unprivileged user namespaces",
                           FAIL if blocked else PASS,
                           "restricted by the kernel" if blocked else "available",
                           detail=knobs,
                           remedy="Firebreak cannot create a sandbox without them."
                                  if blocked else "",
                           evidence=[Evidence("file", ", ".join(knobs), True)]))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("fb.userns", "firebreak", "Unprivileged user namespaces",
                         "none of the kernel knobs is readable"))

    # The real proof: make a namespace. Firebreak's own `check` subcommand does
    # something similar, but it resolves bwrap through PATH, so its answer is
    # not admissible as a security fact here (see the module docstring). This
    # probe uses the trusted absolute path.
    bwrap = host.tool("bwrap")
    if bwrap.usable:
        true_tool = host.tool("true")
        if not true_tool.usable:
            out.append(_skip("fb.namespace", "firebreak", "Sandbox namespace probe",
                             "no trusted /usr/bin/true to run inside the sandbox"))
        else:
            probe = host.run("bwrap", ["--ro-bind", "/usr", "/usr",
                                       "--symlink", "usr/bin", "/bin",
                                       "--symlink", "usr/lib", "/lib",
                                       "--symlink", "usr/lib64", "/lib64",
                                       "--unshare-user", "--unshare-pid", "--unshare-net",
                                       "--proc", "/proc", "--", true_tool.path],
                             timeout=15)
            out.append(Finding("fb.namespace", "firebreak", "Sandbox namespace probe",
                               PASS if probe.ok else FAIL,
                               "a confined process started and exited cleanly" if probe.ok
                               else "bwrap could not start: " + (probe.stderr or "no output"),
                               detail={"exit": probe.code},
                               remedy="" if probe.ok else
                                      "Until this succeeds every Firebreak session will "
                                      "fail; confinement is not partially available.",
                               evidence=[probe.evidence], gated=True))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("fb.namespace", "firebreak", "Sandbox namespace probe",
                         "bwrap is not usable: " + bwrap.reason, gated=True))

    # net=allow needs slirp4netns. Stage B attaches it with
    # --disable-host-loopback so the sandbox reaches the internet but not host
    # loopback or abstract sockets.
    slirp = host.tool("slirp4netns")
    out.append(Finding("fb.netns", "firebreak", "Sandbox NAT (net=allow)",
                       PASS if slirp.usable else WARN,
                       "%s (%s)" % (slirp.path, slirp.trust) if slirp.usable
                       else "unavailable: " + slirp.reason,
                       detail={"path": slirp.path, "trust": slirp.trust},
                       remedy="" if slirp.usable else
                              "Sessions asking for net=allow will fail; net=none still works.",
                       evidence=[Evidence("exec", slirp.path or "|".join(TRUSTED_TOOLS["slirp4netns"]),
                                          slirp.usable, slirp.reason, slirp.trust)]))

    # Honesty: say what is NOT enforced, in the tool, not only in a document.
    out.append(Finding("fb.egress_filtering", "firebreak",
                       "Sandbox egress destination filtering", INFO,
                       "NOT ENFORCED in this release",
                       detail={"enforced": False,
                               "postures": ["none", "allow"],
                               "explanation":
                                   "There are two network postures. net=none gets an "
                                   "empty network namespace. net=allow gets a NAT with "
                                   "--disable-host-loopback, which blocks host loopback "
                                   "and abstract sockets but does NOT restrict which "
                                   "internet destinations the sandbox may reach. "
                                   "--egress-host is RECORDED, not enforced; the session "
                                   "record says so."},
                       evidence=[Evidence("none", "release contract", True)]))

    # Audit state. Firebreak resolves this from passwd, so this tool must too.
    home = host.home()
    if home is None:
        out.append(_skip("fb.audit", "firebreak", "Firebreak audit directory",
                         "this user has no usable passwd entry"))
    else:
        audit = str(home / ".local/state/shadowfetch/firebreak")
        info = host.stat(audit)
        if info is None:
            out.append(Finding("fb.audit", "firebreak", "Firebreak audit directory", INFO,
                               "no sessions have been recorded yet",
                               detail={"path": audit},
                               evidence=[Evidence("file", audit, False, "does not exist")]))
        else:
            mode = stat.S_IMODE(info.st_mode)
            owned = info.st_uid == os.geteuid()
            good = owned and mode == 0o700
            out.append(Finding("fb.audit", "firebreak", "Firebreak audit directory",
                               PASS if good else FAIL,
                               "mode %04o, uid %d" % (mode, info.st_uid),
                               detail={"path": audit, "mode": "%04o" % mode,
                                       "uid": info.st_uid},
                               remedy="" if good else
                                      "Session records must be private to their owner; "
                                      "chmod 700 and check ownership.",
                               evidence=[Evidence("file", audit, True)]))

    relocated = host.env.get("SHADOWFETCH_FIREBREAK_STATE", "").strip()
    out.append(Finding("fb.audit_relocated", "firebreak", "Audit state location",
                       WARN if relocated else PASS,
                       "relocated by SHADOWFETCH_FIREBREAK_STATE" if relocated
                       else "canonical, resolved from the passwd entry",
                       detail={"relocated": bool(relocated)},
                       remedy="Session records for this environment are not going to the "
                              "canonical directory. That is deliberate when a test or an "
                              "isolated session set it; it is a finding otherwise."
                              if relocated else "",
                       evidence=[Evidence("probe", "SHADOWFETCH_FIREBREAK_STATE", True,
                                          "set" if relocated else "unset")],
                       gated=True))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: credential broker (ADR-0011)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

BROKER_PATHS = ("/usr/lib/shadowfetch/broker", "/usr/bin/shadowfetch-brokerd",
                "/usr/lib/systemd/user/shadowfetch-brokerd.service")


def check_broker(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    """The honest answer here is "it does not exist yet".

    ADR-0011 specifies a host-side broker that holds every credential and binds
    one socket into the sandbox, so no token is ever set in the sandbox
    environment. That is NOT what 4.0.0 does: Firebreak still injects granted
    credential values with --setenv at the boundary. A doctor that printed a
    green "broker" line because a directory happened to exist would be
    reporting a control that is not there.
    """
    present = [path for path in BROKER_PATHS if host.exists(path)]
    if not present:
        return [Finding("broker.present", "broker", "Credential broker", INFO,
                        "not implemented in this release",
                        detail={"implemented": False,
                                "credential_delivery": "Firebreak --setenv at the "
                                                       "sandbox boundary",
                                "design": "ADR-0011",
                                "consequence": "A granted credential VALUE is present "
                                               "inside the sandbox for the life of the "
                                               "session. Grant narrowly."},
                        evidence=[Evidence("file", ", ".join(BROKER_PATHS), True,
                                           "none present")])]
    # A partial broker is worse than none: something may route credentials to a
    # component that is not running.
    complete = len(present) == len(BROKER_PATHS)
    return [Finding("broker.present", "broker", "Credential broker",
                    INFO if complete else FAIL,
                    "present" if complete else "PARTIALLY installed",
                    detail={"present": present,
                            "missing": [p for p in BROKER_PATHS if p not in present]},
                    remedy="" if complete else
                           "Some broker components are installed and some are not. "
                           "Credential routing must not be half-configured.",
                    evidence=[Evidence("file", ", ".join(BROKER_PATHS), True)])]


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: APT
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

SHADOWFETCH_KEYRING = "/usr/share/keyrings/shadowfetch.gpg"
APT_CODENAME = "umbra"
DEB_LINE = re.compile(r"^\s*deb(?:-src)?\s+(?P<rest>\S.*)$", re.MULTILINE)


def _apt_source_files(host: Host) -> list[str]:
    files = ["/etc/apt/sources.list"]
    files += host.glob("/etc/apt/sources.list.d", "*.list")
    files += host.glob("/etc/apt/sources.list.d", "*.sources")
    return [path for path in files if host.exists(path)]


def check_apt(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []

    # W-19: [trusted=yes] disables signature checking for every package a
    # repository serves. This is a gate check, not advice.
    offenders, inspected = [], []
    for path in _apt_source_files(host):
        text, ev = host.read_text(path, 1 << 18)
        if text is None:
            continue
        inspected.append(path)
        for match in DEB_LINE.finditer(text):
            entry = match.group("rest")
            if "trusted=yes" in entry.replace(" ", ""):
                offenders.append({"file": path, "entry": entry.strip()})
        if "Trusted: yes" in text or "Trusted:yes" in text:
            offenders.append({"file": path, "entry": "deb822 Trusted: yes"})
    if inspected:
        out.append(Finding("apt.trusted_yes", "apt", "APT signature checking",
                           FAIL if offenders else PASS,
                           "%d source entry/entries disable signature checking"
                           % len(offenders) if offenders
                           else "every source entry is signature-checked",
                           detail={"offenders": offenders, "files": inspected},
                           remedy="Replace [trusted=yes] with [signed-by=%s]."
                                  % SHADOWFETCH_KEYRING if offenders else "",
                           evidence=[Evidence("file", ", ".join(inspected), True)],
                           gated=True))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("apt.trusted_yes", "apt", "APT signature checking",
                         "no APT source file could be read", gated=True))

    keyring = host.exists(SHADOWFETCH_KEYRING)
    out.append(Finding("apt.keyring", "apt", "Shadowfetch repository key",
                       PASS if keyring else FAIL,
                       "present" if keyring else "missing: " + SHADOWFETCH_KEYRING,
                       remedy="" if keyring else
                              "Without it the Shadowfetch repository cannot be verified.",
                       evidence=[Evidence("file", SHADOWFETCH_KEYRING, keyring)],
                       gated=True))

    # Valid-Until. A repository nobody republishes expires and every installed
    # machine loses `apt update` -- which is precisely what happened when the
    # index was published with a 14-day window.
    indexes = [path for path in host.glob("/var/lib/apt/lists", "*InRelease")
               if APT_CODENAME in path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]]
    if not indexes:
        out.append(_skip("apt.validity", "apt", "Shadowfetch index validity",
                         "no cached InRelease for %s in /var/lib/apt/lists" % APT_CODENAME))
    else:
        worst = None
        for path in indexes:
            text, ev = host.read_text(path, 1 << 20)
            if not text:
                continue
            for line in text.splitlines():
                if line.startswith("Valid-Until:"):
                    stamp = line.split(":", 1)[1].strip()
                    try:
                        when = datetime.datetime.strptime(
                            stamp, "%a, %d %b %Y %H:%M:%S %Z").replace(
                                tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)
                    except ValueError:
                        continue
                    remaining = (when - datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)).total_seconds()
                    if worst is None or remaining < worst[0]:
                        worst = (remaining, path, stamp, ev)
                    break
        if worst is None:
            out.append(_skip("apt.validity", "apt", "Shadowfetch index validity",
                             "no parseable Valid-Until in the cached index"))
        else:
            remaining, path, stamp, ev = worst
            days = remaining / 86400
            status = FAIL if remaining <= 0 else (WARN if days < 7 else PASS)
            out.append(Finding("apt.validity", "apt", "Shadowfetch index validity", status,
                               "expired %s" % stamp if remaining <= 0
                               else "valid for %.1f more day(s)" % days,
                               detail={"valid_until": stamp, "seconds_remaining": int(remaining),
                                       "index": path},
                               remedy="apt update will refuse this index. The repository "
                                      "must be republished." if status == FAIL else "",
                               evidence=[ev], gated=True))

    audit = host.run("dpkg", ["--audit"], timeout=20)
    if audit.evidence.ok:
        clean = audit.ok and not audit.stdout
        out.append(Finding("apt.dpkg", "apt", "Package database", PASS if clean else FAIL,
                           "consistent" if clean else "needs repair",
                           detail={"output": audit.stdout.splitlines()[:20]},
                           remedy="" if clean else
                                  "Run sudo dpkg --configure -a, then shadowfetch-update.",
                           evidence=[audit.evidence]))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("apt.dpkg", "apt", "Package database", audit.evidence.note,
                         [audit.evidence]))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: services
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

SHADOWFETCH_SYSTEM_UNITS = (
    "fireproofd.service",
    "shadowfetch-firewatchd.service",
    "shadowfetch-hwscan.service",
    "shadowfetch-ember.service",
    "grub-btrfsd.service",
)


def check_services(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []

    failed = host.run("systemctl", ["--failed", "--no-legend", "--plain", "--no-pager"],
                      timeout=15)
    if failed.evidence.ok:
        units = [line.split()[0] for line in failed.stdout.splitlines() if line.strip()]
        out.append(Finding("svc.failed", "services", "Failed units",
                           FAIL if units else PASS,
                           "%d failed unit(s)" % len(units),
                           detail={"units": units},
                           remedy="systemctl status <unit> for each." if units else "",
                           evidence=[failed.evidence]))
    else:
        out.append(_skip("svc.failed", "services", "Failed units", failed.evidence.note,
                         [failed.evidence]))

    # One call, not one per unit: `is-active` prints a line per argument, and
    # its non-zero exit for "inactive" is an ANSWER, not a failure to look.
    probe = host.run("systemctl", ["is-active", *SHADOWFETCH_SYSTEM_UNITS], timeout=15)
    if not probe.evidence.ok:
        out.append(_skip("svc.shadowfetch", "services", "Shadowfetch units",
                         probe.evidence.note, [probe.evidence]))
    else:
        answers = probe.stdout.splitlines()
        states = {unit: (answers[index].strip() if index < len(answers) else "unknown")
                  for index, unit in enumerate(SHADOWFETCH_SYSTEM_UNITS)}
        broken = sorted(unit for unit, state in states.items() if state == "failed")
        out.append(Finding("svc.shadowfetch", "services", "Shadowfetch units",
                           FAIL if broken else PASS,
                           "%d failed: %s" % (len(broken), ", ".join(broken)) if broken
                           else "no Shadowfetch unit is in a failed state",
                           detail={"states": states},
                           remedy="systemctl status <unit>." if broken else "",
                           evidence=[probe.evidence]))

    # Persistent journald. ADR-0010 mirrors every audit row to the journal so
    # the chain head leaves the user's trust domain. With volatile storage that
    # mirror dies at reboot and the off-uid copy stops existing -- which makes
    # this a security finding, not a logging preference.
    persistent = host.is_dir("/var/log/journal")
    out.append(Finding("svc.journal_persistent", "services", "Persistent journal",
                       PASS if persistent else FAIL,
                       "/var/log/journal exists" if persistent
                       else "journald storage is volatile",
                       remedy="" if persistent else
                              "The off-uid audit mirror does not survive a reboot. "
                              "Create /var/log/journal and restart systemd-journald.",
                       evidence=[Evidence("file", "/var/log/journal", persistent)],
                       gated=True))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: recovery
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def check_recovery(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []

    rootfs = ""
    text, ev = host.read_text("/proc/mounts")
    if text:
        for line in text.splitlines():
            parts = line.split()
            if len(parts) >= 3 and parts[1] == "/":
                rootfs = parts[2]
                break
    if not text:
        out.append(_skip("rec.btrfs", "recovery", "Snapshot-capable root", ev.note, [ev]))
    else:
        btrfs = rootfs == "btrfs"
        out.append(Finding("rec.btrfs", "recovery", "Snapshot-capable root",
                           PASS if btrfs else WARN,
                           "root is %s" % (rootfs or "unknown"),
                           detail={"fstype": rootfs},
                           remedy="" if btrfs else
                                  "Rollback recovery needs btrfs; checkpoints and "
                                  "snapshots are unavailable on this root.",
                           evidence=[ev]))

    snapshots = host.glob("/.snapshots", "*") if host.is_dir("/.snapshots") else []
    if host.is_dir("/.snapshots"):
        out.append(Finding("rec.snapshots", "recovery", "System snapshots",
                           PASS if snapshots else WARN,
                           "%d snapshot entry/entries" % len(snapshots),
                           detail={"count": len(snapshots)},
                           remedy="" if snapshots else
                                  "No snapshot exists to roll back to.",
                           evidence=[Evidence("file", "/.snapshots", True)]))
    else:
        out.append(Finding("rec.snapshots", "recovery", "System snapshots", WARN,
                           "/.snapshots does not exist",
                           remedy="Snapper is not configured; there is nothing to roll "
                                  "back to.",
                           evidence=[Evidence("file", "/.snapshots", False)]))

    recovery_sources = host.glob("/usr/share/shadowfetch/apt-recovery", "*.sources")
    out.append(Finding("rec.apt_sources", "recovery", "Offline APT recovery sources",
                       PASS if recovery_sources else WARN,
                       "%d recovery source file(s)" % len(recovery_sources),
                       detail={"files": recovery_sources},
                       remedy="" if recovery_sources else
                              "Phoenix cannot restore APT sources without them.",
                       evidence=[Evidence("file", "/usr/share/shadowfetch/apt-recovery",
                                          bool(recovery_sources))]))

    checkpoint = host.tool("checkpoint")
    out.append(Finding("rec.checkpoint", "recovery", "Checkpoint engine",
                       PASS if checkpoint.usable else WARN,
                       "%s (%s)" % (checkpoint.path, checkpoint.trust)
                       if checkpoint.usable else "unavailable: " + checkpoint.reason,
                       remedy="" if checkpoint.usable else
                              "Firebreak sessions will run without an undo point.",
                       evidence=[Evidence("exec",
                                          checkpoint.path or "|".join(TRUSTED_TOOLS["checkpoint"]),
                                          checkpoint.usable, checkpoint.reason,
                                          checkpoint.trust)]))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: security
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

HARDENING_SYSCTL = "/etc/sysctl.d/99-shadowfetch-hardening.conf"
HARDENING_MODPROBE = "/etc/modprobe.d/99-shadowfetch-hardening.conf"
SECURE_BOOT_EFIVAR = ("/sys/firmware/efi/efivars/"
                      "SecureBoot-8be4df61-93ca-11d2-aa0d-00e098032b8c")


def check_security(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []

    # DECLARED vs ENFORCED. The shipped .conf file is a request; /proc/sys is
    # what the kernel is doing. A check that only asserts the file exists
    # would have passed on every machine where the setting never applied.
    declared, ev = host.read_text(HARDENING_SYSCTL, 1 << 16)
    if declared is None:
        out.append(_skip("sec.sysctl", "security", "Kernel hardening applied",
                         ev.note, [ev], gated=True))
    else:
        wanted = _kv(declared)
        mismatched, unreadable = [], []
        for key, value in sorted(wanted.items()):
            live, _ = host.read_text("/proc/sys/" + key.replace(".", "/"), 128)
            if live is None:
                unreadable.append(key)
            elif live.split() and live.split()[0] != value:
                mismatched.append({"key": key, "declared": value, "actual": live.split()[0]})
        status = FAIL if mismatched else (WARN if unreadable else PASS)
        out.append(Finding("sec.sysctl", "security", "Kernel hardening applied", status,
                           "%d of %d declared sysctls differ from the running kernel"
                           % (len(mismatched), len(wanted)) if mismatched
                           else "every declared sysctl matches the running kernel",
                           detail={"declared": len(wanted), "mismatched": mismatched,
                                   "unreadable": unreadable},
                           remedy="Run sudo sysctl --system; the shipped hardening is "
                                  "declared but not in force." if mismatched else "",
                           evidence=[ev, Evidence("file", "/proc/sys/<key>", True)],
                           gated=True))

    declared, ev = host.read_text(HARDENING_MODPROBE, 1 << 16)
    modules, mev = host.read_text("/proc/modules")
    if declared is None or modules is None:
        out.append(_skip("sec.modprobe", "security", "Disabled modules stay unloaded",
                         (ev if declared is None else mev).note, [ev, mev]))
    else:
        blocked = [line.split()[1] for line in declared.splitlines()
                   if line.strip().startswith("install ") and len(line.split()) >= 2]
        loaded = {line.split()[0] for line in modules.splitlines() if line.split()}
        breached = sorted(set(blocked) & loaded)
        out.append(Finding("sec.modprobe", "security", "Disabled modules stay unloaded",
                           FAIL if breached else PASS,
                           "%d module(s) that should be disabled are loaded: %s"
                           % (len(breached), ", ".join(breached)) if breached
                           else "none of the %d disabled modules is loaded" % len(blocked),
                           detail={"declared_blocked": blocked, "loaded_anyway": breached},
                           remedy="Something loaded a module the hardening config disables."
                                  if breached else "",
                           evidence=[ev, mev]))

    data, ev = host.read_bytes(SECURE_BOOT_EFIVAR, 16)
    if data is None:
        out.append(_skip("sec.secure_boot", "security", "Secure Boot",
                         ev.note + " (usually means BIOS boot or no permission)", [ev]))
    else:
        enabled = len(data) >= 5 and data[4] == 1
        out.append(Finding("sec.secure_boot", "security", "Secure Boot",
                           PASS if enabled else INFO,
                           "enabled" if enabled else "disabled",
                           detail={"enabled": enabled}, evidence=[ev]))

    # Agent state must be private to its owner: it holds session records and
    # mission history.
    home = host.home()
    if home is None:
        out.append(_skip("sec.state_perms", "security", "Agent state permissions",
                         "this user has no usable passwd entry"))
    else:
        target = str(home / ".local/state/shadowfetch")
        info = host.stat(target)
        if info is None:
            out.append(Finding("sec.state_perms", "security", "Agent state permissions",
                               INFO, "no agent state directory yet",
                               detail={"path": target},
                               evidence=[Evidence("file", target, False, "does not exist")]))
        else:
            mode = stat.S_IMODE(info.st_mode)
            private = not (mode & 0o077) and info.st_uid == os.geteuid()
            out.append(Finding("sec.state_perms", "security", "Agent state permissions",
                               PASS if private else FAIL,
                               "mode %04o, uid %d" % (mode, info.st_uid),
                               detail={"path": target, "mode": "%04o" % mode},
                               remedy="" if private else
                                      "Session records and mission history are readable "
                                      "by others; chmod go-rwx.",
                               evidence=[Evidence("file", target, True)]))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Checks: development state
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

#: Environment variables that relocate or relax something. Their presence is
#: not an error -- a developer or a test sets them deliberately -- but a
#: support bundle from a machine where one is set describes a system that is
#: not the shipped one, and that has to be visible at the top of the report.
POSTURE_ENV = (
    "SHADOWFETCH_FIREBREAK_STATE",
    "SHADOWFETCH_AGENT_WORKSPACES",
    "SHADOWFETCH_ELEMENT",
    "SHADOWFETCH_FIREBREAK_TEST_BIN",
    "SHADOWFETCH_CHECKPOINT_BIN",
    "SHADOWFETCH_MISSIONS_STATE",
    "SHADOWFETCH_PROVIDERS_DIR",
)

SHADOWFETCH_BINARIES = (
    "shadowfetch-firebreak", "shadowfetch-checkpoint", "shadowfetch-mcp",
    "shadowfetch-missions", "shadowfetch-health", "shadowfetch-doctor",
    "shadowfetch-facts", "shadowfetch-passport", "shadowfetch-gpu",
    "shadowfetch-update", "shadowfetch-workbench", "shadowfetch-recovery",
)


def check_development(host: Host) -> list[Finding]:
    out: list[Finding] = []

    overrides = {name: host.env[name] for name in POSTURE_ENV if host.env.get(name)}
    out.append(Finding("dev.overrides", "development", "Posture overrides in effect",
                       WARN if overrides else PASS,
                       "%d override(s) set" % len(overrides) if overrides
                       else "no posture override is set",
                       detail={"overrides": sorted(overrides)},
                       remedy="This environment is not the shipped configuration. Say so "
                              "in the bug report." if overrides else "",
                       evidence=[Evidence("probe", "os.environ", True,
                                          "names of set overrides")],
                       gated=True))

    # /usr/local/bin shadows /usr/bin on a default PATH, so a copy there
    # silently replaces the packaged tool for every interactive invocation.
    shadowed = [path for path in
                ("/usr/local/bin/" + name for name in SHADOWFETCH_BINARIES)
                if host.exists(path)]
    out.append(Finding("dev.shadowed", "development", "Unpackaged tool copies",
                       FAIL if shadowed else PASS,
                       "%d Shadowfetch tool(s) shadowed from /usr/local/bin" % len(shadowed)
                       if shadowed else "no Shadowfetch tool is shadowed",
                       detail={"paths": shadowed},
                       remedy="A copy in /usr/local/bin takes precedence over the packaged "
                              "tool on a default PATH. Remove it or accept that this "
                              "machine is not running the release." if shadowed else "",
                       evidence=[Evidence("file", "/usr/local/bin/shadowfetch-*",
                                          True)],
                       gated=True))

    # Every packaged tool should be distro-managed. One that is not is either a
    # developer build or a substituted binary, and the two look identical from
    # here -- which is the finding.
    classifications = {}
    for name in ("firebreak", "checkpoint", "missions"):
        tool = host.tool(name)
        classifications[name] = {"path": tool.path, "trust": tool.trust}
    bad = sorted(k for k, v in classifications.items()
                 if v["trust"] not in (Trust.DISTRO_MANAGED, Trust.MISSING))
    out.append(Finding("dev.trust", "development", "Packaged tool provenance",
                       FAIL if bad else PASS,
                       "%s not distro-managed" % ", ".join(bad) if bad
                       else "every resolvable Shadowfetch tool is distro-managed",
                       detail=classifications,
                       remedy="Reinstall from the repository, or record that this is a "
                              "development machine." if bad else "",
                       evidence=[Evidence("probe", "classify_executable", True)],
                       gated=True))

    out.append(Finding("dev.running_from", "development", "This tool's own provenance",
                       INFO, str(Path(__file__).resolve()),
                       detail=dict(zip(("trust", "reason"),
                                       classify_executable(Path(__file__).resolve()))),
                       evidence=[Evidence("probe", "classify_executable(__file__)", True)]))
    return out


CHECKS = {
    "system": check_system,
    "gpu": check_gpu,
    "drivers": check_drivers,
    "ai": check_ai,
    "firebreak": check_firebreak,
    "broker": check_broker,
    "apt": check_apt,
    "services": check_services,
    "recovery": check_recovery,
    "security": check_security,
    "development": check_development,
}


def diagnose(host: Host, categories: Sequence[str] | None = None) -> list[Finding]:
    """Run the selected categories. One category's crash costs one category.

    A doctor that dies on unusual hardware is useless precisely where it is
    most needed, so a collector that raises becomes a FAIL finding naming the
    exception rather than taking the run down.
    """
    selected = list(categories) if categories else list(CHECKS)
    findings: list[Finding] = []
    for name in selected:
        collector = CHECKS.get(name)
        if collector is None:
            continue
        try:
            findings.extend(collector(host))
        except Exception as exc:                       # noqa: BLE001 - see docstring
            findings.append(Finding(name + ".collector", name,
                                    "%s checks" % name.title(), FAIL,
                                    "the collector raised %s: %s"
                                    % (type(exc).__name__, exc),
                                    remedy="This is a bug in shadowfetch-doctor. The "
                                           "other categories are unaffected.",
                                    evidence=[Evidence("none", "internal", False)]))
    return findings


def summarise(findings: Sequence[Finding]) -> dict[str, int]:
    return {status: sum(f.status == status for f in findings) for status in STATUSES}


def report_document(findings: Sequence[Finding], host: Host) -> dict:
    version, _ = host.read_text(VERSION_FILE)
    return {
        "schema": SCHEMA_VERSION,
        "tool": "shadowfetch-doctor",
        "tool_version": VERSION,
        "release": (version or "").strip() or "unknown",
        "generated_utc": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
                                 .isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
        "summary": summarise(findings),
        "findings": [f.as_dict() for f in findings],
    }


GLYPH = {PASS: "OK", WARN: "!!", FAIL: "XX", INFO: "--", SKIP: "??"}


def human_report(document: dict) -> str:
    lines = ["Shadowfetch Doctor %s · release %s · %s"
             % (document["tool_version"], document["release"], document["generated_utc"]),
             "=" * 72]
    current = None
    for finding in document["findings"]:
        if finding["category"] != current:
            current = finding["category"]
            lines.append("")
            lines.append(current.upper())
        lines.append("  [%s] %s: %s" % (GLYPH[finding["status"]], finding["title"],
                                        finding["summary"]))
        if finding.get("remedy"):
            lines.append("       -> " + finding["remedy"])
    counts = document["summary"]
    lines += ["", "=" * 72,
              "%d pass · %d warn · %d fail · %d info · %d NOT INSPECTED"
              % (counts[PASS], counts[WARN], counts[FAIL], counts[INFO], counts[SKIP])]
    if counts[SKIP]:
        lines.append("A '??' line was not inspected. It is not a pass.")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def exit_code(findings: Sequence[Finding]) -> int:
    counts = summarise(findings)
    if counts[FAIL]:
        return 2
    if counts[WARN]:
        return 1
    return 0


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Release check
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def release_check(host: Host, expect_version: str | None = None) -> tuple[dict, int]:
    """The installed-system counterpart to the build-time release gates.

    ``make source-gate`` / ``package-gate`` / ``iso-gate`` prove things about
    the tree and the image. Nothing proved anything about a machine that has
    the image installed. This does, over the subset of findings marked
    ``gated``.

    Only PASS and INFO are acceptable. A gated FAIL blocks for the obvious
    reason; a gated SKIP blocks because "we could not tell" and "it is fine"
    are different answers and only one of them is a pass; a gated WARN blocks
    because the shipped configuration has no warnings -- a warning here means
    this machine is not what the release says it is.
    """
    findings = [f for f in diagnose(host) if f.gated]
    if expect_version is not None:
        actual = ""
        text, ev = host.read_text(VERSION_FILE)
        if text:
            actual = text.strip()
        findings.append(Finding("rel.version", "system", "Expected release version",
                                PASS if actual == expect_version else FAIL,
                                "%s (expected %s)" % (actual or "unknown", expect_version),
                                detail={"expected": expect_version, "actual": actual},
                                evidence=[ev], gated=True))

    blocking = [f for f in findings if f.status not in (PASS, INFO)]
    document = {
        "schema": SCHEMA_VERSION,
        "tool": "shadowfetch-doctor",
        "mode": "release-check",
        "tool_version": VERSION,
        "generated_utc": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
                                 .isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
        "passed": not blocking,
        "rule": "only pass and info clear the gate; fail, warn and skip all block. "
                "'not inspected' is not a pass",
        "summary": summarise(findings),
        "blocking": [f.as_dict() for f in blocking],
        "findings": [f.as_dict() for f in findings],
    }
    return document, (0 if not blocking else 2)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Support bundle
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

class RedactorUnavailable(Exception):
    """The one shared redactor could not be loaded from a trusted location."""


#: Where sf_redact.py may be loaded from. Absolute, and the file is classified
#: before it is imported: loading a module is executing it, so a substitutable
#: sf_redact.py is a substitutable redactor AND arbitrary code.
REDACTOR_DIRS = ("/usr/lib/shadowfetch/missions",)


def load_redactor(extra_dirs: Sequence[str] = ()):
    """Import the shared redactor, or raise.

    There is deliberately no fallback. A second, weaker redactor is exactly
    the thing that drifts out of step with the real rules and then silently
    stops catching a shape the real one catches -- and the failure mode is a
    credential in a bug report, which cannot be undone. Refusing to write a
    bundle is recoverable; shipping an unredacted one is not.

    ``extra_dirs`` exists for the source tree, where the module lives under
    packages/. Those directories are classified exactly like the packaged one,
    so a developer tree that anyone else can write is refused too.
    """
    import importlib.util

    tried = []
    for directory in (*REDACTOR_DIRS, *extra_dirs):
        candidate = Path(directory) / "sf_redact.py"
        if not candidate.is_file():
            tried.append("%s: not present" % candidate)
            continue
        trust, reason = classify_executable_readable(candidate)
        if trust not in (Trust.DISTRO_MANAGED, Trust.USER_MANAGED, Trust.DEVELOPER):
            tried.append("%s: refused (%s)" % (candidate, reason))
            continue
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("sf_redact_for_doctor", candidate)
        if spec is None or spec.loader is None:
            tried.append("%s: not importable" % candidate)
            continue
        module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        try:
            spec.loader.exec_module(module)
        except Exception as exc:                        # noqa: BLE001
            tried.append("%s: import failed (%s)" % (candidate, exc))
            continue
        if not hasattr(module, "redact_bytes") or not hasattr(module, "PLACEHOLDER"):
            tried.append("%s: does not provide redact_bytes" % candidate)
            continue
        return module, str(candidate), trust
    raise RedactorUnavailable(
        "No usable sf_redact.py. A support bundle is not written without the "
        "shared redactor; there is no fallback by design. Tried: " + "; ".join(tried))


def classify_executable_readable(path: Path) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Trust classification for a file that is imported rather than executed.

    Identical to ``classify_executable`` except that the executable bit is not
    required: a Python module is not chmod +x, but who can write it matters
    just as much, because importing it runs it.
    """
    try:
        resolved = path.resolve()
        info = resolved.stat()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return Trust.MISSING, "%s does not exist" % path
    except OSError as exc:
        return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s cannot be inspected: %s" % (path, exc)
    if not stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
        return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s is not a regular file" % resolved
    uid = os.getuid()
    root_owned = True
    for component in (resolved, *resolved.parents):
        try:
            cinfo = component.stat()
        except OSError as exc:
            return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s cannot be inspected: %s" % (component, exc)
        problem = _substitutable_by_others(cinfo, uid)
        if problem is not None:
            return Trust.UNTRUSTED, "%s is %s" % (component, problem)
        if cinfo.st_uid != 0:
            root_owned = False
    if str(resolved).startswith(PACKAGED_EXEC_PREFIXES) and root_owned:
        return Trust.DISTRO_MANAGED, "%s is root-owned in a packaging-owned directory" % resolved
    if root_owned:
        return Trust.DEVELOPER, "%s is root-owned outside the packaging directories" % resolved
    return Trust.USER_MANAGED, "%s is writable only by root and uid %d" % (resolved, uid)


#: The standing refusals, reproduced verbatim into every MANIFEST so the person
#: about to send a bundle can read the policy rather than infer it from the
#: file list.
NEVER_COLLECTED = (
    "Credential values of any kind, including provider API keys and tokens. "
    "Only credential NAMES are recorded, because the identity is the audit "
    "trail and the value is the secret.",
    "Private keys, PEM material, SSH keys and /etc/ssh.",
    "Browser profiles, cookies, saved passwords and history.",
    "Shell history of any shell.",
    "Any file under a home directory except the Firebreak audit directory, "
    "and that only when --include-sessions was passed.",
    "Any process environment block (/proc/*/environ).",
    "/etc/shadow, /etc/gshadow and /etc/ssl/private.",
    "Documents, media, mail, or anything else the user owns.",
)


@dataclass
class BundleItem:
    path: str
    source: str
    collector: str
    bytes: int
    sha256: str
    note: str = ""


class Bundle:
    """The single write path into a support archive.

    Everything -- including the MANIFEST -- goes through ``add``, and ``add``
    applies the shared redactor to every byte with no opt-out. That is what
    makes "the bundle is redacted" a property of the code rather than a
    promise about every call site.
    """

    def __init__(self, redactor, redactor_path: str, redactor_trust: str):
        self._redactor = redactor
        self.redactor_path = redactor_path
        self.redactor_trust = redactor_trust
        self.items: list[BundleItem] = []
        self.skipped: list[dict] = []
        self._blobs: dict[str, bytes] = {}

    def add(self, path: str, data: bytes | str, *, source: str, collector: str,
            note: str = "") -> None:
        if isinstance(data, str):
            data = data.encode("utf-8", "replace")
        # The one and only redaction point. Do not add another write path.
        clean = self._redactor.redact_bytes(data)
        self._blobs[path] = clean
        self.items.append(BundleItem(
            path=path, source=source, collector=collector, bytes=len(clean),
            sha256=hashlib.sha256(clean).hexdigest(), note=note))

    def skip(self, path: str, source: str, reason: str) -> None:
        self.skipped.append({"path": path, "source": source, "reason": reason})

    def manifest(self, *, include_sessions: bool, host: Host) -> dict:
        return {
            "schema": SCHEMA_VERSION,
            "tool": "shadowfetch-doctor",
            "tool_version": VERSION,
            "created_utc": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
                                   .isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
            "redactor": {
                "module": self.redactor_path,
                "trust": self.redactor_trust,
                "applied_to": "every byte in this archive, including this manifest",
                "placeholder": self._redactor.PLACEHOLDER,
                "limits": "sf_redact removes shapes it knows. Read its module "
                          "docstring for what it does NOT catch before treating "
                          "this bundle as safe for a public issue tracker.",
            },
            "inspected_root": str(host.root),
            "include_sessions": include_sessions,
            "hashes": "sha256 of the bytes in this archive, i.e. AFTER redaction",
            "self": {
                "path": "MANIFEST.json",
                "note": "This file. It is redacted like every other member but "
                        "is absent from 'collected' below, because a hash "
                        "cannot cover the file it is written into. Every OTHER "
                        "member of the archive is listed there.",
            },
            "collected": [vars(item) for item in self.items],
            "skipped": self.skipped,
            "never_collected": list(NEVER_COLLECTED),
        }

    def write(self, destination: Path, *, include_sessions: bool,
              host: Host) -> tuple[Path, dict]:
        """Write the archive and return the manifest that is INSIDE it.

        Returning the embedded document rather than rebuilding one afterwards
        matters: a second call would describe an archive with one more member
        than the one on disk, and the summary the user is shown would not be a
        summary of the file they are about to send.
        """
        document = self.manifest(include_sessions=include_sessions, host=host)
        # The manifest is added like any other item, so it is redacted too --
        # a source path can itself contain a secret-shaped string.
        self.add("MANIFEST.json", json.dumps(document, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n",
                 source="generated", collector="manifest")
        destination = destination.expanduser()
        # 0600 from the moment it exists: a bundle is a support artefact, not
        # something to leave world-readable in /tmp while it is being written.
        flags = os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL
        handle = os.open(str(destination), flags, 0o600)
        with os.fdopen(handle, "wb") as raw:
            with tarfile.open(fileobj=raw, mode="w:gz") as archive:
                for path in sorted(self._blobs):
                    blob = self._blobs[path]
                    info = tarfile.TarInfo("shadowfetch-support/" + path)
                    info.size = len(blob)
                    info.mode = 0o600
                    info.mtime = int(datetime.datetime.now(
                        datetime.timezone.utc).timestamp())
                    info.uid = info.gid = 0
                    info.uname = info.gname = "root"
                    archive.addfile(info, io.BytesIO(blob))
        return destination, document


#: What a bundle collects, as data. A reviewer can read this table and know
#: the whole of it; there is no collector hiding in a branch somewhere.
FILE_COLLECTORS = (
    ("system/os-release", "/etc/os-release"),
    ("system/version", VERSION_FILE),
    ("system/kernel", "/proc/version"),
    ("system/cmdline", "/proc/cmdline"),
    ("system/meminfo", "/proc/meminfo"),
    ("system/mounts", "/proc/mounts"),
    ("system/modules", "/proc/modules"),
    ("system/tainted", "/proc/sys/kernel/tainted"),
    ("security/sysctl-hardening.conf", HARDENING_SYSCTL),
    ("security/modprobe-hardening.conf", HARDENING_MODPROBE),
    ("apt/sources.list", "/etc/apt/sources.list"),
)

#: journalctl is restricted to Shadowfetch units. The full journal contains
#: every other application's logs, which are not ours to put in a bug report.
JOURNAL_UNITS = SHADOWFETCH_SYSTEM_UNITS

#: Caps on Firebreak session records, newest first. A working machine
#: accumulates thousands: an uncapped bundle from the build host collected
#: 1,423 session files, which is both unusable as a bug report and a much
#: larger exposure than the problem being reported needs. The cap and the
#: number withheld both go in the MANIFEST, so a truncated bundle says it is
#: truncated rather than looking complete.
MAX_SESSIONS_COLLECTED = 25
MAX_SESSIONS_LISTED = 200


def _sessions_newest_first(host: Host, audit: str) -> list[str]:
    """Session record paths, most recently modified first.

    Newest first because the session someone is filing a bug about is almost
    always the last one they ran.
    """
    paths = host.glob(audit, "*.session")
    def when(path):
        info = host.stat(path)
        return info.st_mtime if info else 0.0
    return sorted(paths, key=when, reverse=True)


def build_bundle(destination: Path, host: Host, *, include_sessions: bool = False,
                 categories: Sequence[str] | None = None,
                 extra_redactor_dirs: Sequence[str] = ()) -> tuple[Path, dict]:
    """Collect a redacted support bundle. Raises RedactorUnavailable to refuse."""
    module, redactor_path, redactor_trust = load_redactor(extra_redactor_dirs)
    bundle = Bundle(module, redactor_path, redactor_trust)

    document = report_document(diagnose(host, categories), host)
    bundle.add("doctor.json", json.dumps(document, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n",
               source="shadowfetch-doctor --json", collector="doctor")
    bundle.add("doctor.txt", human_report(document) + "\n",
               source="shadowfetch-doctor", collector="doctor")

    for name, source in FILE_COLLECTORS:
        try:
            text, evidence = host.read_text(source, 1 << 20)
        except Refused as exc:
            bundle.skip(name, source, "refused by the collection allowlist: %s" % exc)
            continue
        if text is None:
            bundle.skip(name, source, evidence.note)
            continue
        bundle.add(name, text, source=source, collector="file")

    for source in _apt_source_files(host):
        if source == "/etc/apt/sources.list":
            continue
        name = "apt/sources.list.d/" + source.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        text, evidence = host.read_text(source, 1 << 18)
        if text is None:
            bundle.skip(name, source, evidence.note)
        else:
            bundle.add(name, text, source=source, collector="file")

    # PCI inventory from sysfs: ids and bound driver only. No serial numbers,
    # no MAC addresses, no device paths beyond the bus slot.
    devices = _pci_devices(host)
    bundle.add("hardware/pci.json",
               json.dumps(devices, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n",
               source="/sys/bus/pci/devices/*/{class,vendor,device,driver}",
               collector="sysfs",
               note="ids and bound driver only; no serials, MACs or device paths")

    # Environment: NAMES for everything, VALUES only for the posture variables
    # whose value is the finding. Everything still passes through the redactor.
    env_document = {
        "names": sorted(host.env),
        "posture_values": {name: host.env[name] for name in POSTURE_ENV
                           if host.env.get(name)},
        "credential_identities_present": sorted(
            name for name in CREDENTIAL_IDENTITY_NAMES if host.env.get(name)),
        "policy": "names only; a value appears here ONLY for the posture "
                  "variables listed in POSTURE_ENV, never for a credential",
    }
    bundle.add("environment.json",
               json.dumps(env_document, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n",
               source="os.environ", collector="environment",
               note="names only, plus posture-variable values")

    for name, args, unit_note in (
        ("systemctl", ["--failed", "--no-legend", "--plain", "--no-pager"],
         "failed units"),
        ("dpkg-query", ["-W", "-f=${Package} ${Version} ${Status}\\n", "shadowfetch-*"],
         "Shadowfetch packages only"),
    ):
        outcome = host.run(name, args, timeout=20)
        target = "services/%s.txt" % name
        if outcome.evidence.ok:
            bundle.add(target, outcome.stdout + "\n", source=outcome.evidence.source,
                       collector="exec", note=unit_note)
        else:
            bundle.skip(target, outcome.evidence.source, outcome.evidence.note)

    journal_args = ["--no-pager", "-b", "-n", "300", "-o", "short-iso"]
    for unit in JOURNAL_UNITS:
        journal_args += ["-u", unit]
    outcome = host.run("journalctl", journal_args, timeout=30)
    if outcome.evidence.ok:
        bundle.add("services/journal.txt", outcome.stdout + "\n",
                   source=outcome.evidence.source, collector="exec",
                   note="this boot, Shadowfetch units only, last 300 lines")
    else:
        bundle.skip("services/journal.txt", outcome.evidence.source, outcome.evidence.note)

    # Firebreak session records. OFF by default: a session manifest quotes the
    # agent command and the workspace paths, which is the most useful thing in
    # the bundle and also the most personal. Opt in, and it is still redacted.
    home = host.home()
    audit = None if home is None else str(home / ".local/state/shadowfetch/firebreak")
    if audit is None:
        bundle.skip("firebreak/", "passwd", "this user has no usable passwd entry")
    else:
        found = _sessions_newest_first(host, audit)
        limit = MAX_SESSIONS_COLLECTED if include_sessions else MAX_SESSIONS_LISTED
        chosen, withheld = found[:limit], max(0, len(found) - limit)
        listing = []
        for path in chosen:
            info = host.stat(path)
            listing.append({"name": path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1],
                            "bytes": info.st_size if info else None,
                            "mode": "%04o" % stat.S_IMODE(info.st_mode) if info else None})
        bundle.add("firebreak/sessions-listing.json",
                   json.dumps({"total_present": len(found), "listed": len(listing),
                               "withheld": withheld, "order": "newest first",
                               "sessions": listing}, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n",
                   source=audit, collector="listing",
                   note="names, sizes and modes only")
        if not include_sessions:
            bundle.skip("firebreak/sessions/", audit,
                        "session contents not collected: --include-sessions was not "
                        "passed (%d record(s) present)" % len(found))
        else:
            for path in chosen:
                text, evidence = host.read_text(path, 1 << 20)
                name = "firebreak/sessions/" + path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
                if text is None:
                    bundle.skip(name, path, evidence.note)
                else:
                    bundle.add(name, text, source=path, collector="file",
                               note="collected because --include-sessions was passed")
            if withheld:
                bundle.skip("firebreak/sessions/", audit,
                            "%d older session record(s) withheld: --include-sessions "
                            "collects the %d most recent, and this bundle says so "
                            "rather than looking complete"
                            % (withheld, MAX_SESSIONS_COLLECTED))

    return bundle.write(destination, include_sessions=include_sessions, host=host)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# CLI
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def _default_bundle_path() -> Path:
    stamp = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ")
    return Path.cwd() / ("shadowfetch-support-%s.tar.gz" % stamp)


def _repo_redactor_dirs() -> list[str]:
    """The source tree's copy, for running this tool from a checkout.

    Same classification as the packaged path, so a checkout in a directory a
    third party can write is refused rather than imported.
    """
    return [str(parent / "packages/shadowfetch-missions/data/usr/lib/shadowfetch/missions")
            for parent in Path(__file__).resolve().parents]


def main(argv: Sequence[str] | None = None) -> int:
    release_version, _ = Host().read_text(VERSION_FILE)
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="shadowfetch-doctor",
        description="Structured diagnosis, redacted support bundles, and an "
                    "installed-system release check.",
        epilog="A '??' result was NOT inspected. It is not a pass, and it fails "
               "--release-check.")
    parser.add_argument("--version", action="version",
                        version="shadowfetch-doctor (Shadowfetch Linux) %s"
                                % ((release_version or "").strip() or "unknown"))
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="emit structured JSON")
    parser.add_argument("--category", action="append", choices=CATEGORIES, default=[],
                        metavar="NAME",
                        help="limit to a category (repeatable): " + ", ".join(CATEGORIES))
    parser.add_argument("--timeout", type=int, default=8, metavar="SECONDS",
                        help="per-subprocess timeout (default 8)")

    mode = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    mode.add_argument("--support", action="store_true",
                      help="write a redacted support bundle (.tar.gz) for a bug report")
    mode.add_argument("--release-check", action="store_true",
                      help="verify this installed system against the release contract; "
                           "exits 2 if any gated check fails OR could not run")

    parser.add_argument("--output", metavar="FILE",
                        help="where to write the support bundle")
    parser.add_argument("--include-sessions", action="store_true",
                        help="include Firebreak session records in the bundle. They "
                             "quote agent commands and workspace paths. Off by default.")
    parser.add_argument("--expect-version", metavar="VERSION",
                        help="release-check: require this exact release version")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    host = Host(timeout=args.timeout)

    if args.release_check:
        document, code = release_check(host, args.expect_version)
        if args.json:
            print(json.dumps(document, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        else:
            print("Shadowfetch release check: %s"
                  % ("PASSED" if document["passed"] else "FAILED"))
            for finding in document["blocking"]:
                print("  [%s] %s: %s" % (GLYPH[finding["status"]], finding["title"],
                                         finding["summary"]))
            if not document["blocking"]:
                print("  %d gated check(s), none blocking."
                      % len(document["findings"]))
            else:
                print("\n  Only pass and info clear this gate. A '??' result blocks it "
                      "on purpose: a release is not signed off on facts nobody "
                      "established.")
        return code

    if args.support:
        destination = Path(args.output) if args.output else _default_bundle_path()
        try:
            written, manifest = build_bundle(
                destination, host, include_sessions=args.include_sessions,
                categories=args.category or None,
                extra_redactor_dirs=_repo_redactor_dirs())
        except RedactorUnavailable as exc:
            print("shadowfetch-doctor: refusing to write a support bundle.\n%s" % exc,
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        except FileExistsError:
            print("shadowfetch-doctor: %s already exists; refusing to overwrite it."
                  % destination, file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        except OSError as exc:
            print("shadowfetch-doctor: could not write %s: %s" % (destination, exc),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        if args.json:
            print(json.dumps({"bundle": str(written), "manifest": manifest},
                             indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        else:
            print("Support bundle: %s" % written)
            print("  %d item(s) collected, %d skipped, redacted by %s"
                  % (len(manifest["collected"]), len(manifest["skipped"]),
                     manifest["redactor"]["module"]))
            print("  MANIFEST.json inside the archive lists exactly what is in it.")
            if not args.include_sessions:
                print("  Firebreak session contents were NOT included "
                      "(pass --include-sessions to include them).")
            print("  Read it before you send it: redaction removes shapes it knows, "
                  "not everything.")
        return 0

    findings = diagnose(host, args.category or None)
    document = report_document(findings, host)
    print(json.dumps(document, indent=2, sort_keys=True) if args.json
          else human_report(document))
    return exit_code(findings)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
